A funnel census found 476,702 admissible cells that had never been judged -- 87% of the docket.
They were not lost, broken or rejected; nothing had ever looked at them. The desk's own
JUDGE_COVERAGE.json has published `unjudged_total` and `oldest_unjudged_age_h` per family for as
long as it has existed, and NO ORGAN READ EITHER. That is the same shape as the other defects
found today: 1,196 placement refusals nobody read, 837 rows retired against an empty canon, 158
certificates voided by a tuple comparison. The evidence existed; nothing was watching it.

THIS FENCE READS THE STALL, NOT ITS CAUSE -- the principle check_placement_interlock.py
established this morning, for the same reason: the next time judging stops it will be for a
reason nobody has thought of yet, and a fence keyed to today's reason would miss it as
thoroughly as no fence at all.

A BACKLOG IS NOT A BREACH; A STALLED BACKLOG IS, and that distinction is the whole design. The
desk will hold a large backlog for as long as its miners outproduce its judge, and a fence that
fired on size alone would be red the day it was written -- which the interlock's own docstring
